          "en": "# Law No. 27 of 2022 on Personal Data Protection\n1.  Whereas personal data protection is one of the human rights forming part of the protection of the self, so that a legal foundation is necessary in order to provide security over personal data, based on the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;\n2.  Whereas personal data protection is intended to guarantee the right of citizens to the protection of the self and to foster awareness in society, as well as to guarantee the recognition of and respect for the importance of personal data protection;\n3.  Whereas provisions on personal data are at present found in several laws and regulations, so that in order to improve the effectiveness of the implementation of personal data protection, regulation on personal data protection in a Law is necessary;\n4.  Whereas, based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to enact a Law on Personal Data Protection;\nArticle 5 paragraph (1), Article 20, Article 28G paragraph (1), Article 28H paragraph (4) and Article 28J of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;\nWith the Joint Approval of\nTHE HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF THE REPUBLIC OF INDONESIA and THE PRESIDENT OF THE REPUBLIC OF INDONESIA\nLAW ON PERSONAL DATA PROTECTION.\nSufficiently clear.\nSufficiently clear.\nLetter a\nThe term \"protection principle\" means that every processing of Personal Data is carried out by providing protection to the Personal Data Subject in respect of his Personal Data and ensuring that such Personal Data is not misused.\nLetter b The term \"legal certainty principle\" means that every processing of Personal Data is carried out based on a legal basis in order to realise Personal Data Protection and all matters supporting its implementation, so as to obtain legal recognition within and outside the court.\nLetter c The term \"public interest principle\" means that in upholding Personal Data Protection, the public interest or the interests of society at large must be taken into account. Such public interest includes, among others, the interests of state administration and of national defence and security.\nLetter d The term \"principle of benefit\" means that the regulation of Personal Data Protection must be beneficial to the national interest, in particular in realising the ideal of general welfare.\nLetter e The term \"principle of prudence\" means that the parties involved in the processing and supervision of Personal Data must give attention to all aspects that have the potential to cause loss.\nLetter f The term \"principle of balance\" means an effort in Personal Data Protection to balance the right to Personal Data on the one hand and the legitimate rights of the State based on the public interest on the other hand.\nLetter g The term \"principle of accountability\" means that all parties involved in the processing and supervision of Personal Data act responsibly so as to be able to guarantee the balance of the rights and obligations of the parties involved, including the Personal Data Subject.\nLetter h The term \"principle of confidentiality\" means that Personal Data is protected from unauthorised parties and/or from unlawful Personal Data processing activities.\nParagraph 1\nLetter a\nPersonal Data of a specific nature is Personal Data which, when processed, may result in a greater impact on the Personal Data Subject, including discriminatory treatment and greater loss to the Personal Data Subject.\nLetter b Sufficiently clear.\nParagraph 2\nLetter a\nThe term \"health data and information\" means records or information of an individual relating to physical health, mental health and/or health services.\nLetter b The term \"biometric data\" means data relating to the physical, physiological or behavioural characteristics of an individual that enables"
